<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Cornovii</h1><p class="st-meta">50 BCE – 60 CE (approx.) · capital Viroconium Cornoviorum (Wroxeter)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Iron Age people of the West Midlands and Welsh borders, centred on the Wrekin, whose Roman town of Viroconium became one of the largest in Britain.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Cornovii occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a> and the Welsh borderland in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. Their landscape was dominated by hillforts such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-wrekin">Wrekin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titterstone-clee-hill">Titterstone Clee</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breiddin">Breiddin</a>, many of which had been occupied for centuries before the Roman period.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They issued no coins and used very little pottery, so their society is known mainly from hillforts and farmsteads. Livestock farming was central, and the brine springs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a> supplied salt that was traded in distinctive clay containers. No rulers are named in any source, suggesting power was held by local chiefs.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Roman army advanced into the region in the 50s, and around 55 to 60 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress was built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viroconium-cornoviorum">Wroxeter</a> below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-wrekin">Wrekin</a>, which seems to have been abandoned. When the army left, the site became Viroconium Cornoviorum, capital of their civitas in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain</a> and later the seat of post-Roman rulers in what became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cornovii.jpg" alt="Cornovii" width="320" height="464"><figcaption>Image: (myself) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Britain.north.peoples.Ptolemy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>50 BCE – 60 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Viroconium Cornoviorum (Wroxeter)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cornovii (Midlands)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=5&amp;focus=cornovii" aria-label="Cornovii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4621 362 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cornovii lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4941 618L5304 942M4941 618L5020 640M4941 618L4910 602"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5020 640h0M4910 602h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4941 618h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4941 618h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Viroconium Cornoviorum. <a href="/atlas?year=5&amp;focus=cornovii">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
